      <h2>Approach</h2>
      <p>Four disciplines govern the work:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Human and machine in dialogue.</strong> Computational methods extend
          comparative reach; human scholarship and community authority constrain
          interpretation. Neither substitutes for the other.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Home-language first.</strong> A concept is studied in the speech and
          practice that made it, not as an English keyword with a foreign costume.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No premature equivalence.</strong> We do not retire a term by finding
          its “nearest” English virtue. The remainder is the research.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Separate illustration from claim.</strong> Early catalog notes are
          labeled as examples. Formal papers will carry sources. We will not invent
          citations to look finished.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        We are not a branding studio for national character, and we do not collect
        untranslatable words as souvenirs. The catalog and the research pillars are
        working instruments for people who must speak and think across real difference.
      </p>
